 FINAL SOLUTION:
2025-12-01 10:02:42,328 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Here’s a concise “what’s hot” map of current ML trends with concrete papers, models, and datasets you can use right away. I grouped items by theme and suggested what to explore depending on your project focus.

1) Reasoning-centric LLMs and test-time learning
- Why it matters: Strong push toward models that reason more reliably (math, code, planning) and can improve at test time.
- Papers to see
  - ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (Wang et al.). Single-LLM framework that mixes in-context learning with test-time RL; a small open model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new bounds on classic optimization problems. Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve
  - RefineBench: Evaluating Refinement Capability of Language Models via Checklists (benchmarks iterative self-refinement).
- Models to try
  - deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1 and distills (1.5B–32B): strong open reasoning baselines for chain-of-thought–style tasks.
  - microsoft/Phi-4-reasoning: compact, well-documented “reasoning” variant of Phi-4.
  - nvidia/Nemotron-Research-Reasoning-Qwen-1.5B: small reasoning-specialized model.
- Evaluate with
  - TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro (harder than original MMLU; arXiv:2406.01574)
  - Idavidrein/gpqa (adversarial science QA; arXiv:2311.12022)
  - math-ai/aime25 (AIME-style math)
- Worth exploring
  - If you need small, fast models with good reasoning, start with Phi-4-reasoning or DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-7B/14B and benchmark on MMLU-Pro/GPQA/AIME.

2) Multimodal/video reasoning shifts from “think about” to “think with” the video
- Why it matters: New work shows current MLLMs over-rely on language priors; interactive, grounded reasoning brings real gains.
- Papers to see
  - Video-R2 (Maaz et al.): introduces metrics TAC (Think–Answer Consistency) and VAS (Video Attention Score) to quantify grounded reasoning; uses timestamp-aware SFT + GRPO with a Temporal Alignment Reward to boost visual grounding and temporal coherence.
  - Video-CoM (Rasheed et al.): “Chain of Manipulations” (actively re-watch/refocus videos) + reasoning-aware RL; +3.6% average over recent SOTA on 9 video reasoning benchmarks with surprisingly little data. Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
- Models to try
  - OpenGVLab/InternVL3-8B (strong general VLM baseline)
  - Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct (robust, widely used VL stack)
  - llava-hf/LLaVA-NeXT-Video-7B-hf (video QA baseline)
- Datasets/challenges to consider
  - “World in a Frame”: culture-mixing benchmark shows LVLMs struggle with complex cultural co-occurrence.
  - “From Pixels to Feelings”: aligning MLLMs with human affective perception.
  - CaptionQA: benchmark for “can captions substitute images” across retrieval/agentic pipelines.
- Worth exploring
  - If your app needs reliable visual grounding or video QA, prototype with Qwen3-VL-8B or InternVL3-8B, then port ideas from Video-CoM (iterative visual actions) and Video-R2 (temporal alignment) for measurable gains.

3) Long context is moving from token windows to real task competence
- Why it matters: Big windows are common; the frontier is demonstrating faithful retrieval, reasoning, and manipulation over long inputs.
- Datasets/benchmarks
  - zai-org/LongBench-v2: long-context, realistic multi-task evaluation emphasizing deep reasoning (arXiv:2412.15204).
  - rbiswasfc/ruler: synthetic but precise probes (variable tracking, multi-hop aggregation, multi-key needle-in-haystack) to check what “long context” truly works.
- Models to consider
  - Qwen3 family (e.g., Qwen/Qwen3-4B-Instruct-2507; Qwen/Qwen3-Next-80B lines) and Llama-3.2 variants with extended contexts.
- Worth exploring
  - Don’t just check max window—measure with LongBench v2/RULER to ensure your long-context use cases (e.g., contract analysis, long logs) actually improve.

4) Generative models: faster sampling, better control, and new conditional formalisms
- Why it matters: Diffusion remains dominant; faster/one-step pathways and finer control are rapidly improving.
- Papers to see
  - Adversarial Flow Models: unify adversarial generative models and flows; support one-step or multi-step generation via adversarial training.
  - Vision Bridge Transformer (ViBT): learns Brownian bridge dynamics for efficient conditional generation (alternative to standard diffusion).
  - Decoupled DMD: advances diffusion distillation by decoupling CFG from distribution matching for stable few-/single-step generators.
  - OmniRefiner: reinforcement-guided local diffusion to preserve fine-grained details in reference-based editing.
  - Fast3Dcache: speeds up diffusion-based 3D generation by caching; reduces denoising iterations without retraining.
  - REASONEDIT: adds explicit reasoning/planning to instruction-driven image editing for complex multi-step edits.
  - Captain Safari: long, 3D-consistent, camera-controlled video generation under aggressive motion.
  - AnyTalker: scalable, identity-aware diffusion transformer for multi-person talking video with minimal multi-person data.
- Ready-to-try models/spaces
  - ali-vilab/modelscope-damo-text-to-video-synthesis and ali-vilab/text-to-video-ms-1.7b for text-to-video baselines (Hugging Face).
- Worth exploring
  - If you need higher throughput or controllability, experiment with diffusion distillation (Decoupled DMD) or bridge/flow models for faster sampling; for 3D or editing tasks, test Fast3Dcache/OmniRefiner/REASONEDIT ideas.

5) Agents and tool-using systems: toward robust, purposeful interaction
- Why it matters: Tool use and environment interaction now emphasize efficiency and internalization (fewer redundant steps, better planning).
- Papers/benchmarks
  - WMAct: “Thinking by Doing” (reward rescaling + interaction frequency annealing) converts multi-turn tasks to single-turn successes in Sokoban/Maze/Taxi; transfers to harder environments.
  - SWE-bench Verified (princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified): curated 500 human-validated issues for autonomous code agents.
  - LiveCodeBench (livecodebench/code_generation_lite): up-to-date code gen benchmark with execute/test tracks.
  - Agent benchmarks/spaces on Hugging Face (e.g., smolagents/computer-use-agent) if you want quick demos.
- Worth exploring
  - For coding/ops agents, evaluate on SWE-bench Verified and LiveCodeBench; incorporate WMAct-like training signals (purposeful actions, turn annealing). Track contamination and leakage carefully (see below).

6) Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) for scale-efficiency and robustness
- Why it matters: Sparse activation gives big-model quality with manageable compute.
- What’s new
  - Mixtral-8x7B/-8x22B remain strong open MoE baselines.
  - “YOLO Meets MoE”: adaptive expert routing improves robustness in detection—MoE is spreading beyond LLMs.
  - Qwen3 MoE variants appear in the ecosystem.
- Worth exploring
  - If your workload is bursty or multilingual/multi-domain, MoE can deliver strong quality-per-dollar; Mixtral-8x7B-Instruct-v0.1 is a great starting point.

7) Data hygiene, fairness, and evaluation pitfalls (especially for vision/video)
- Why it matters: Small leaks or cultural biases can inflate scores or break generalization.
- Papers to see
  - Find the Leak, Fix the Split: cluster-based frame grouping prevents near-duplicate leakage across splits in video datasets.
  - World in a Frame: culture-mixing challenge reveals VLM failures with co-occurring cultural elements.
  - From Pixels to Feelings: aligning MLLMs with human cognitive/affective perception (beyond object recognition).
- Worth exploring
  - Apply cluster-based splitting for vision/video; add culture-mix probes; include affective/human-perception checks if relevant to user-facing apps.

8) RAG quality and evaluation is maturing
- Why it matters: Many RAG apps plateau due to retrieval errors, poor chunking, or weak evaluation.
- Datasets/metrics
  - neural-bridge/rag-dataset-12000 (starter QA for RAG systems).
  - RAGAS-based evaluations are common in the open community; you can find small RAGAS-formatted sets on Hugging Face.
  - Hallucination-focused sets exist (e.g., neural-bridge/rag-hallucination-dataset-1000).
- Worth exploring
  - Focus on retrieval quality, granularity, and faithfulness metrics (RAGAS; correctness and support). Consider hybrid retrieval and domain-specific embeddings (e.g., Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B) for better recall/precision.

Starter kits (models + datasets to get traction fast)
- Reasoning-heavy assistant
  - Models: deepseek-ai/DeepSeek-R1-Distill-Qwen-14B or 32B; microsoft/Phi-4-reasoning (smaller).
  - Eval: TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, Idavidrein/gpqa, math-ai/aime25.
- Multimodal visual QA (images)
  - Models: OpenGVLab/InternVL3-8B, Qwen/Qwen3-VL-8B-Instruct.
  - Probes: culture-mixing, “From Pixels to Feelings,” and your domain images.
- Video QA
  - Models: llava-hf/LLaVA-NeXT-Video-7B-hf as a baseline; consider Video-CoM training approach for interactive reasoning.
  - Ideas: apply TAC/VAS-style diagnostics from Video-R2 to verify grounding.
- Long-context analysis (documents/logs)
  - Models: recent Qwen3 or Llama-3.2 variants with long windows.
  - Eval: zai-org/LongBench-v2, rbiswasfc/ruler.
- Coding/automation agent
  - Models: strong general model + code toolchain; consider MoE (Mixtral-8x7B) if budget allows.
  - Eval: princeton-nlp/SWE-bench_Verified; livecodebench/code_generation_lite (and execution tracks).
- RAG app (enterprise docs)
  - Embeddings: Qwen/Qwen3-Embedding-0.6B.
  - Dataset/eval: neural-bridge/rag-dataset-12000; RAGAS-based scoring; consider hallucination-focused tests.

Notable specialized/experimental directions
- Domain MLLMs: OralGPT-Omni (dental imaging + language) shows strong results are possible even with scarce data via thoughtful curation and multimodal alignment.
- Text-to-video and control: ali-vilab/modelscope… pipelines are good fast baselines; AnyTalker pushes multi-person, audio-driven realism with identity-aware attention.
- Distillation and speedups: Decoupled DMD for one/few-step diffusion; Fast3Dcache for 3D; OmniRefiner for detail-preserving edits.
- New conditional generative formulations: Adversarial Flow Models and ViBT (bridge dynamics) are promising if you’re exploring fast, high-quality conditional generation.

How I combined sources
- Papers: arXiv searches surfaced Video-R2, Video-CoM, WMAct, ThetaEvolve, and recent generative/control methods.
- Models: Hugging Face model registry (DeepSeek-R1 family, Phi-4, Qwen3/Qwen2.5 VL, InternVL3, LLaVA-NeXT Video, Mixtral).
- Datasets/benchmarks: Hugging Face datasets for MMLU-Pro, GPQA, LongBench v2, RULER, SWE-bench Verified, LiveCodeBench, and RAG evaluation sets.
- Spaces/tools: Agent demos and text-to-video spaces on Hugging Face to quickly prototype.

If you share your project’s target modality (text/code/image/video), constraints (latency/budget), and evaluation needs, I can turn this into a short customized stack (model + training/eval recipe) with concrete setup steps.
