  - Problem: MLLMs for video often produce convincing but logically inconsistent or weakly visual-grounded reasoning, relying on linguistic priors.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces two metrics—Think Answer Consistency (TAC) for alignment of reasoning with answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) for reliance on visual vs textual cues. Across 11 video reasoning benchmarks, current models rely heavily on language priors.
  - Method: Dual-step post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO reinforcement learning guided by a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
  - Results: Video-R2 improves TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; enhances trustworthy video understanding. Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al.)
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” passively after encoding, causing a semantic bottleneck; they can’t rewatch/refocus/verify evidence for fine-grained spatiotemporal reasoning.
  - Method: Interactive Video Reasoning that “thinks with videos.” Video-CoM executes a Chain of Manipulations (CoM): iterative visual actions to gather/refine evidence. Trained with Video CoM Instruct (18K instruction-tuning examples) + reinforcement learning via reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks, +3.6% average over recent SOTA while using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples. Ablations show reasoning-aware rewards improve accuracy and interpretability.
  - Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (Shu et al.)
  - Problem: Rigid, structured reasoning in multi-turn interaction hinders active learning and efficient world-model internalization for LLM agents.
  - Method (WMAct): Enables “thinking through doing” with two mechanisms: (1) reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and incentivize purposefulness; (2) interaction frequency annealing to progressively cap allowed turns, forcing internalization of dynamics and reducing over-reliance on environment feedback.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, WMAct enables solving tasks in a single turn that previously required multiple interactions; shows strong transfer to more complex environments and improvements on a suite of reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhong et al.)
  - Problem: Audio-driven multi-person talking video generation suffers from limited diverse data and difficulty maintaining coherent multi-identity interactivity.
