1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
- Problem: Video reasoning traces are often convincing but logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visual evidence.
- Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to quantify alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to measure reliance on visual vs textual cues. Finds current models overuse linguistic priors across 11 benchmarks.
- Method: Two-step post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + GRPO reinforcement learning guided by a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporal precision and causal coherence.
- Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple video reasoning benchmarks.
- Availability: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

2) Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
- Problem: Most MLLMs “think about” videos in text after a single pass, leading to shallow spatiotemporal reasoning.
- Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—models “think with videos” via a Chain of Manipulations (CoM), iteratively acting on visual inputs to gather/verify evidence.
- Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K instructions) for multi-step manipulation; SFT plus RL with reasoning-aware GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
- Results: Strong performance on 9 benchmarks, +3.6% average over recent SOTA while using only ~25K SFT and 3K GRPO videos. Improved accuracy and interpretability shown in ablations.
- Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

3) Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
- Goal: Foster robust world-model internalization in LLM agents through active, efficient interaction (WMAct).
- Key mechanisms: (1) Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interaction; (2) Interaction frequency annealing to progressively limit turns, encouraging internalization rather than reliance on environment feedback.
- Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, achieves single-turn solutions previously requiring multi-turn and shows strong transfer across reasoning benchmarks.

4) AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
- Challenge: Multi-person, audio-driven generation is hampered by limited multi-person data and difficulty ensuring coherent multi-speaker interactivity.
- Architecture: Extensible multi-stream framework; identity-aware attention in a Diffusion Transformer block that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary scaling of identities.
- Training: Primarily on single-person videos to learn speaking patterns; interactivity refined with a small set of real multi-person clips.
- Evaluation: New metric and dataset for naturalness and interactivity. Demonstrates strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity with favorable data efficiency and identity scalability.

5) ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (cs.LG; cs.CL)
- Contribution: Open-source framework extending AlphaEvolve with a single LLM and test-time RL, enabling continual learning on open optimization problems.
- Techniques: Large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties to avoid stagnation, and optional reward shaping; scales both in-context learning and RL at test time.
- Results: Small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality; across 2 models/4 tasks, test-time RL outperforms inference-only baselines and yields faster progress/generalization.
- Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

