  - Introduces RapunSL, a quantum separation logic extending QSL with two new connectives—linear combination and mixing—enabling scalable, sound reasoning.
  - Establishes basis-locality (reduce superposition reasoning to pure states) and outcome-locality (reduce mixed states from measurement to pure states), with separation interpreted as disentanglement and frame rule providing entanglement-local specs.
  - Shows substantial scalability gains on challenging case studies.

- 2511.23470v1 — Spectral analysis of the Koopman operator recovers Hamiltonian parameters in open quantum systems (quant-ph/math-ph, 2025-11-28)
  - Data-driven identification of Hamiltonian parameters from first-moment dynamics using the Koopman operator spectrum via multichannel HAVOK (mHAVOK).
  - Validated on an open 2D quantum harmonic oscillator: accurately recovers oscillation frequencies, damping rates, Kerr shifts, Jaynes–Cummings coupling strengths, and time-dependent frequency modulations; most parameters within 5% of ground truth.
  - Outperforms Fourier and matrix-pencil methods under strong dissipation, highlighting Koopman theory’s practicality for quantum dynamics.

- 2511.23468v1 — On valuation deviations and their geometry (math.MG, 2025-11-28)
  - Introduces two valuation-based deviations on convex bodies and constructs associated “intrinsic” pseudometrics.
  - Proves results linking geometric properties of these induced deviations to information about the underlying valuations.

