--- Summary of Round 3 ---
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'SWE-bench benchmark 2024 2025', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4636 tokens): - Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in MLLMs (cs.CV)
  - Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs can be logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visuals; models over-rely on linguistic priors.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to align reasoning with answers and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues.
  - Method: Timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to improve temporal precision and causal coherence.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across 11 video reasoning benchmarks; code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
  - Paradigm: “Think with videos” by iteratively manipulating visual evidence (Chain of Manipulations, CoM), overcoming the passive “think about videos” bottleneck.
  - Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; trained with 25K SFT and 3K GRPO samples.
  - RL: Reasoning-aware GRPO with step-level rewards (not just sparse answer rewards) to enforce grounded, consistent reasoning.
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks, +3.6% over recent SOTA with far fewer samples; code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
  - Goal: Enable LLM agents to internalize environment dynamics efficiently through active interaction.
  - WMAct: Two mechanisms—(1) reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and encourage purposeful interaction; (2) interaction frequency annealing to progressively cap interaction turns, pushing internalization of dynamics.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, and Taxi, solves tasks in single turns that previously needed multiple; exhibits strong transfer to more complex environments.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
  - Architecture: Extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention to iteratively process identity–audio pairs, scaling to arbitrary numbers of identities.
  - Training: Learns multi-person speaking patterns using only single-person videos, then refines interactivity with a small number of real multi-person clips.
  - Evaluation: New metric and dataset for naturalness and interactivity.
  - Results: Strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity; favorable trade-off between data cost and scalability.

- A Lyapunov-Based Small-Gain Theorem for Fixed-Time Stability (eess.SY)
  - Contribution: A small-gain theorem for interconnected systems where each subsystem admits a fixed-time ISS Lyapunov function (FxT-ISS); a nonlinear small-gain condition ensures the whole interconnection is fixed-time stable (FxTS).
  - Scope: Bridges gaps between asymptotic/finite-time small-gain theorems and fixed-time stability.
  - Examples: Analytical and numerical demonstrations, including fixed-time feedback optimization without time-scale separation.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (cs.LG; cs.CL)
  - Framework: Open-source, test-time evolving system that scales in-context learning and RL to learn strategies while improving bounds on open optimization problems.
  - Features: Single-LLM pipeline, large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties to avoid stagnation, optional reward shaping for stability.
  - Results: A small open model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and the first auto-correlation inequality; across 4 tasks and 2 models, RL at test-time beats inference-only; RL checkpoints generalize faster and better to trained and unseen tasks.
  - Code: https://github.com/ypwang61/ThetaEvolve

- RapunSL: Untangling Quantum Computing with Separation, Linear Combination and Mixing (cs.PL)
  - Logic: Extends Quantu
Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'LiveCodeBench 2024 2025', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4636 tokens): Here are concise summaries of the 10 papers, preserving key contributions, methods, metrics, and outcomes:

- Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
  Authors: Muhammad Maaz; Hanoona Rasheed; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
  Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs often look convincing but are logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visuals.
  Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs textual cues. Analysis on 11 benchmarks shows heavy reliance on linguistic priors.
  Method: Post-training with timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning + Group Relative Policy Optimization (GRPO) using a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporal alignment and causal coherence.
  Results: Consistent gains in TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; more trustworthy video understanding. Code/dataset/model to be open-sourced.
  Link: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23478v1

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
  Authors: Hanoona Rasheed; Mohammed Zumri; Muhammad Maaz; Ming-Hsuan Yang; Fahad Shahbaz Khan; Salman Khan
  Paradigm: From “thinking about videos” (static context) to “thinking with videos” via interactive visual actions.
  Model: Video-CoM executes a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) to iteratively gather/refine evidence.
  Data/Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; SFT on 25K samples and GRPO on 3K with step-level reasoning rewards (not just sparse answer rewards).
  Results: +3.6% average improvement over recent SOTA on nine video reasoning benchmarks with far fewer training samples; better accuracy and interpretability via reasoning-aware rewards.
  Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM
  Link: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23477v1

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
  Authors: Bao Shu; Yan Cai; Jianjian Sun; et al.
  Goal: Build robust, efficient world-model reasoning without rigid, structured chains.
  Method (WMAct): 
    1) Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundant interactions.
    2) Interaction frequency annealing to progressively limit turns, pushing internalization of dynamics.
  Results: Solves Sokoban, Maze, Taxi tasks in single turns that previously needed multiple; strong transfer to complex environments; improves reasoning benchmarks.
  Link: http://arxiv.org/abs/2511.23476v1

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
