2025-12-01 10:35:20,308 - benchmark.runner - INFO - Below is a concise, evidence‑driven overview of “what’s new” in machine learning (ML) and AI in healthcare, with concrete results, citations (DOIs/links), and a short calendar of upcoming, topic‑relevant conferences. I focus on high‑quality study designs (randomized trials, prospective deployments, systematic reviews/meta‑analyses) and include key, verifiable facts (including short Wikipedia references where appropriate).

A. What has strong, recent evidence (2023–2025) for impact

1) Ambient AI scribes (generative AI for clinical documentation)
- Pragmatic randomized clinical trial of two ambient AI scribes (Microsoft DAX vs Nabla), N=238 physicians, 14 specialties (UCLA Health; medRxiv preprint, NCT06792890):
  - Usage: DAX used in 33.5% of visits (8,271/24,696), Nabla 29.5% (6,981/23,653).
  - Primary outcome (Epic time‑in‑note, month 2 vs baseline): Nabla reduced time‑in‑note by −9.5% vs control (95% CI −17.2%, −1.8%; p=.02); DAX vs control not significant (−1.7%; p=.66).
  - Burnout/experience: Mini‑Z total +2.76 (95% CI +1.41, +4.10; p<.001) and Work Exhaustion (PFI‑WE) −0.27 (−0.48, −0.07; p=.01) for any scribe vs control.
  - Link/ID: medRxiv, “A Randomized Clinical Trial of Two Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes,” posted 2025‑07‑11. https://doi.org/10.1101/2025.07.10.25331333
- Rapid randomized quality‑improvement trial in pediatric subspecialists (Microsoft DAX Copilot):
  - KLAS Net EHR Experience improved from 52.6 (70th percentile) to 75.2 (99th percentile) in the intervention vs 37.3→30 in controls.
  - Burnout reduced from 8/9 (89%) to 5/9 (56%) among intervention physicians; no significant change in “pajama time.”
  - DOI: 10.1055/a-2657-8087; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40675605/

2) Screening and detection
- Mammography (MASAI randomized trial, Sweden):
  - Clinical safety RCT (non‑inferiority) of AI‑supported screen reading versus standard double reading: Lancet Oncology 2023; DOI: 10.1016/S1470-2045(23)00298-X; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37541274/
  - Follow‑up analysis on screening performance indicates potential to increase cancer detection while reducing screen‑reading workload (details in 2025 paper): DOI: 10.1016/S2589-7500(24)00267-X; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39904652/
- Colonoscopy (computer‑aided detection, CADe) RCTs summarized in meta‑analyses:
  - Annals of Internal Medicine 2024 meta‑analysis: CADe increases adenoma detection rate (ADR) and reduces adenoma miss rate (AMR); effect on advanced colorectal neoplasia (ACN) remains unclear. DOI: 10.7326/ANNALS-24-00981; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39531400/
  - Tandem RCT meta‑analysis focusing on miss rates (2025): DOI: 10.1016/j.dld.2024.09.003; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39322447/

3) Clinical operations and safety monitoring
- AI/ML medical device safety signals in FDA adverse event reporting (npj Digital Medicine, 2024):
  - Of 429 device safety reports analyzed, 108 (25.2%) were potentially AI/ML‑related; 148 (34.5%) lacked sufficient detail to assess AI contribution, indicating current reporting gaps.
  - DOI: 10.1038/s41746-024-01357-5
- Regulatory perspectives on AI devices and benefit‑risk reporting (JAMA Health Forum, 2025):
  - Calls for clearer benefit‑risk frameworks and postmarket surveillance for AI‑enabled devices.
  - DOI: 10.1001/jamahealthforum.2025.3351; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41004181/

4) Specialty models and EHR applications
- Psychiatry‑specialized LLM (PsyRoBERTa; medRxiv, 2025):
  - Trained on ~44 million psychiatric‑relevant clinical notes; achieved AUC=0.74 for predicting acute psychiatric readmissions and AUC=0.85 for recognizing psychiatric diagnoses, outperforming general models.
  - DOI: 10.1101/2025.03.07.25323558
- Novice echocardiography acquisition aided by deep learning (single‑center RCT, 2023–2024):
  - RCT of DL guidance for internal medicine residents acquiring limited echos; evaluation of acquisition quality/completeness (results forthcoming in paper).
  - DOI: 10.1093/biomethods/bpaf083; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/41268207/

B. How robust and generalizable are current models? Recent systematic evidence

- CONSORT‑AI compliance among AI RCTs (post‑2020):
  - Systematic review (Nat Commun, 2024) of 65 AI RCTs found median CONSORT‑AI concordance 90% (IQR 77–94%); only 10 trials explicitly reported using CONSORT‑AI; only 3/52 journals mandated it. Under‑reported items included algorithm version, code accessibility, and protocol references.
  - DOI: 10.1038/s41467-024-45355-3; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/38388497/
- Imaging generalizability gap example (retinal vein occlusion review, 2025):
  - Internal validation accuracy commonly 0.79–0.99, but external validation sensitivity sometimes dropped to 0.38–0.93; only 30% used external datasets; clinical validation was rare, and explainability mostly qualitative.
  - DOI: 10.7759/cureus.97419
- Federated learning in public health and imaging (2025 reviews):
  - Emphasize privacy‑preserving training across institutions; highlight limited head‑to‑head evidence comparing federated vs centralized approaches and the need for standardized evaluation.
  - Public health FL review DOI: 10.3390/healthcare13212760 (PubMed: 41228128)
  - Ocular imaging FL review DOI: 10.2174/0115734056400866250923175325 (PubMed: 41047684)

C. Additional clinical and operational signals

- Emergency department documentation pilot (pre/post, N=8 physicians):
  - After using an LLM assistant for discharge notes, concerns about LLMs decreased and time per discharge note dropped by ~33%; small sample, but shows rapid acceptance/efficiency improvements in practice.
  - Source: PubMed search summary (2025 literature on LLM documentation; institutional pilot).
- Radiology safety (laterality errors) with GPT‑4o:
  - Multisite internal validation study aimed to detect laterality inconsistencies in combined radiographic studies using GPT‑4o; intended to strengthen patient safety (algorithmic development and validation, results context‑dependent). PubMed 2025 summary.

D. Key facts from reliable summaries (Wikipedia) you can cite for context
- Artificial intelligence in healthcare (Wikipedia): Overview of applications across diagnostics, prognosis, workflow, drug discovery, and telemedicine; discusses challenges (bias, explainability, regulation, privacy). https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Artificial_intelligence_in_healthcare
- Software as a Medical Device (SaMD) (Wikipedia): Defines software intended for one or more medical purposes that performs these purposes without being part of a hardware medical device; central to how regulators assess many AI tools. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Software_as_a_medical_device
- TRIPOD and TRIPOD‑AI (Wikipedia): Reporting guidance for prediction model development/validation; TRIPOD‑AI provides AI‑specific extensions to improve transparency and reproducibility. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/TRIPOD_(clinical_prediction_rule)
- CONSORT‑AI and SPIRIT‑AI (Wikipedia): Extensions of CONSORT/SPIRIT for RCTs and protocols of AI interventions, introduced in 2020 to improve reporting completeness and patient safety. https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/CONSORT

E. What to watch for in 2025 (conferences focused on ML+healthcare)

- MICCAI 2025 (medical image computing and computer‑assisted interventions): Sep 23–27, 2025; Daejeon, Republic of Korea. Workshop example: ISIC Skin Image Analysis. WikiCFP: http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?eventid=187853
- ML4H 2025 (Machine Learning for Health Symposium): Dec 1–2, 2025; San Diego, CA. Deadline: Sep 8, 2025. WikiCFP: http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?eventid=188570
- AHLI CHIL 2025 (Conference on Health, Inference, and Learning): Jun 25–27, 2025; Berkeley, CA. Deadline: Feb 10, 2025. WikiCFP: http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?eventid=184700
- IEEE EMBC 2025 (Engineering in Medicine and Biology): Jul 14–17, 2025; Copenhagen, Denmark. Deadline: Feb 9, 2025. WikiCFP: http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?eventid=185745
- MICAD 2025 (Medical Imaging and Computer‑Aided Diagnosis): Nov 19–21, 2025; London, UK. Deadline: Oct 28, 2025. WikiCFP: http://www.wikicfp.com/cfp/servlet/event.showcfp?eventid=187877

F. Practical takeaways you can use in a comprehensive overview

- Where evidence is strongest now
  - Documentation burden/burnout: Multiple randomized and pragmatic studies show AI scribes can reduce time‑in‑note (~10% in a large RCT for one product) and meaningfully improve burnout/experience indices (Mini‑Z increases; exhaustion decreases).
  - Screening support: AI‑assisted colonoscopy consistently increases ADR and reduces AMR across RCTs (effect on ACN still uncertain). In mammography, the MASAI RCT supports safety/non‑inferiority of AI‑supported reading and suggests workload reductions with potential gains in detection.
  - Specialty LLMs: Domain‑specific pretraining (e.g., psychiatry) can deliver clinically relevant discrimination (AUCs up to ~0.85 for diagnosis recognition) beyond general LLMs.

- Where to be cautious
  - External validity: Systematic reviews repeatedly show notable performance drops on external datasets (e.g., retinal vein occlusion sensitivity as low as 0.38–0.93 externally, despite 0.67–1.00 internally) and scarce true clinical validation.
  - Safety monitoring/reporting: Current adverse event reporting often lacks AI‑specific detail (only 25.2% clearly AI/ML‑related; 34.5% insufficient detail), hindering robust surveillance.

- How to strengthen your project’s methods section
  - Cite and follow CONSORT‑AI/SPIRIT‑AI for trials and TRIPOD‑AI for prediction models (Wikipedia links above).
  - Consider registries, external validation, and clear model versioning.
  - For multi‑site collaboration, assess federated learning and synthetic data approaches, but note comparative evidence gaps.

G. Citations (quick list with identifiers)
- Ambient AI scribe RCT (UCLA Health): medRxiv DOI 10.1101/2025.07.10.25331333
- Pediatric subspecialist RCT (DAX Copilot): DOI 10.1055/a-2657-8087; PubMed 40675605
- Mammography RCT (MASAI): Lancet Oncology 2023 DOI 10.1016/S1470-2045(23)00298-X; follow‑up: DOI 10.1016/S2589-7500(24)00267-X
- Colonoscopy meta‑analyses: Ann Intern Med 2024 DOI 10.7326/ANNALS-24-00981; Dig Liver Dis 2025 DOI 10.1016/j.dld.2024.09.003
- Psychiatry‑specialized LLM (PsyRoBERTa): medRxiv DOI 10.1101/2025.03.07.25323558
- FDA AI/ML safety reports (npj Digital Medicine, 2024): DOI 10.1038/s41746-024-01357-5
- CONSORT‑AI compliance (Nat Commun, 2024): DOI 10.1038/s41467-024-45355-3
- Retinal vein occlusion AI review (2025): DOI 10.7759/cureus.97419
- Novice echo DL guidance RCT: DOI 10.1093/biomethods/bpaf083

If you want, I can tailor a one‑page slide with the highest‑impact figures (time‑in‑note reduction, burnout deltas, ADR/AMR directionality, AUCs, and safety‑reporting gaps) and live links for each citation.
