- Generative AI’s healthcare professional role creep: evaluation of health-related customized GPTs (Front Public Health, 2025; Chu et al.; doi:10.3389/fpubh.2025.1584348)
  - Cross-sectional assessment of publicly accessible, customized GPTs in OpenAI’s GPT store that target health information/assistance for patients and clinicians.
  - Examines accessibility and claimed functionality, flagging potential “role creep” into clinical tasks and the implications for safety, scope, and oversight.

- Mixed reality in pediatric oncologic surgery (npj Digital Medicine, 2025; Bronowicki et al.; doi:10.1038/s41746-025-01638-7)
  - Retrospective analysis (2023–2024) comparing surgeries performed with mixed reality (CarnaLife Holo; n=9) versus conventional approaches across diverse pediatric oncology cases (e.g., pulmonary metastases, bone/soft tissue tumors).
  - Findings: Surgical duration generally comparable; slight increases in some cases; hospitalization times within typical ranges. MR did not significantly prolong procedures or hospital stay. Suggests promise with a need for larger studies.

- Generalizability of FDA-approved AI-enabled medical devices (JAMA Netw Open, 2025; Windecker et al.; doi:10.1001/jamanetworkopen.2025.8052)
  - Focuses on ensuring that performance demonstrated in development/clearance translates to safe, effective use in broader real-world clinical practice.
  - Underscores the need for rigorous external validation and generalizability evidence post-approval.

- Advancements in clinical evaluation and regulatory frameworks for AI-driven SaMD (IEEE OJEMB, 2025; Yang et al.; doi:10.1109/OJEMB.2024.3485534)
  - Reviews rapid AI/ML progress (including generative methods to address sparse data), with emphasis on radiology applications.
  - Summarizes FDA guidelines for performance evaluation and proposed continuous surveillance for AI/ML SaMD; catalogs FDA 510(k) AI/ML devices, key challenges, and proposed solutions across design, development, and monitoring to support safer, more effective personalized care.

- Regulatory/policy challenges for AI-enabled combination devices (Front Med Technol, 2024; Santra et al.; doi:10.3389/fmedt.2024.1473350)
  - Analyzes US (510(k) predicate reliance) and EU (MDR) pathways for AI-enabled combination products, highlighting difficulties with adaptive algorithms, continual updates, model degradation, and bias.
  - Draws lessons from recalls/case studies; proposes adaptive, risk-aware frameworks and global standards to balance innovation with patient safety.

- AI-related safety issues in FDA medical device reports (npj Digital Medicine, 2024; Handley et al.; doi:10.1038/s41746-024-01357-5)
  - Evaluates feasibility of leveraging patient safety reports for AI/ML device oversight in response to the 2023 AI Executive Order.
  - Of 429 reports, 108 (25.2%) were potentially AI/ML-related; 148 (34.5%) lacked enough detail to determine AI/ML contribution. Concludes current reporting is insufficient; calls for more comprehensive safety surveillance infrastructure.

