- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns (DOI: 10.1101/2025.10.28.25338965)
  - Model: US branching process with inter-county mobility; transmission heterogeneity via negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading).
  - Simulations: Stronger superspreading (lower r) slows early spatial invasion and increases between-realization variability.
  - Method: Graph neural network infers r from early spatial spread, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - COVID-19 application (US): Pre-lockdown r=0.50 (95% CI: 0.23–1.20); post-lockdown r=1.3 (0.64–3.18).
  - Contribution: Enables superspreading quantification from population-level spatial data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland: a controlled interrupted time series study (DOI: 10.1101/2025.10.30.25339133)
  - Natural experiment, CITS (2015–2021). Aberdeen: 38 bars extended 1–3 hours (to 3am). Glasgow: 10 nightclubs +1 hour (to 4am). Control: Edinburgh.
  - Primary outcome: weekend night-time alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: crimes.
  - Aberdeen: Significant relative increase in ambulance call-outs (+17.4%; effect size=7.061; 95% CI: 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (effect size=3.187; 95% CI: 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses).
  - Glasgow: No significant, robust effects.
  - Conclusion: Later trading hours increased harms in Aberdeen; effects likely moderated by number, capacity, and type of premises; policy relevance for licensing.

