  - Europe (Italy, England, France): Remarkable longevity is predicted by poverty, low income, shorter life expectancy, higher crime, worse health, higher deprivation, fewer 90+ residents, and residence in remote/overseas territories. Old-age poverty alone explains >50% of regional variation in England/France.
  - Data quality: Only 18% of exhaustively validated supercentenarians have birth certificates (0% in the U.S.); birthdates cluster on days divisible by five.
  - Blue zones: Sardinia, Okinawa, Ikaria align with regions of low income, low literacy, high crime, and short life expectancy.
  - Conclusion: Fraud and clerical error likely drive many “remarkable age” records.

