Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'large language model healthcare 2024 evaluation', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 3102 tokens): Here’s a concise synthesis of the included studies, preserving key aims, methods, results, and implications:

- Healthbots for clinical screening and remote monitoring with mood assessment (scoping review)
  - Core point: Mood assessment is crucial in chronic disease management but rarely integrated into routine healthbot workflows.
  - Insight: Few conversational agents automate mood analysis; emerging multimodal and large language models (LLMs) create opportunities to embed emotional assessment into daily/hybrid care.

- Comparative feasibility of reasoning vs non-reasoning LLMs in gynecologic cancer emergency care
  - Aim: Evaluate feasibility of non-reasoning GPT-4o vs reasoning o3-mini-high to support emergency care decisions for gynecologic cancer patients.
  - Context: Addresses growing interest in LLMs for clinical decision-making; feasibility focus (no results reported in abstract).

- Emergency physicians’ attitudes toward LLM-based documentation (pre/post implementation)
  - Design: 8 emergency physicians (avg 12 years’ experience); surveys at baseline, 3 days, 5 weeks after using an LLM assistant for ED discharge notes.
  - Findings: Concerns about LLMs significantly decreased and stayed low; perceived workload dropped; time per discharge note cut by ~33%.
  - Implication: Rapid, sustained acceptance and efficiency gains; first real-world evidence tracking attitude shifts over time in clinical settings.

- Multimodal LLMs for chest X-ray analysis with incomplete context
  - Aim: Test whether combining text+image inputs improves accuracy and interpretability of chest radiography impressions when reports lack complete data.
  - Focus: Robustness to incomplete contextual information and mitigation of performance loss via multimodality.

- Perceptions of portable dentistry in Asia using machine learning
  - Design: Cross-sectional, multi-country (Jun–Nov 2024); 1,550 patients and 1,320 dentists from Pakistan, India, Bangladesh, Iran, Saudi Arabia; validated questionnaires; ML models (RF, GB, KNN, SVM, Logistic Regression) evaluated on accuracy, precision, recall, F1, ROC-AUC; qualitative thematic analysis of open responses.
  - Key results: 76% of patients and 74% of dentists reported need for portable dentistry; highest interest among urban residents, women, and younger dentists. Random Forest performed best (up to 97.10% accuracy for patients; 88.17% for dentists).
  - Drivers/challenges: Acceptance influenced by demographics, professional experience, perceived demand. Benefits include improved access, reduced travel, personalized care; challenges include equipment and operational constraints.
  - Recommendations: Awareness campaigns, training, telehealth integration, and economic feasibility analyses to support sustainable implementation.

- Hybrid ambient clinical documentation (ACD) and physician performance
  - Context: After-hours EHR “work outside of work” (WOW) contributes to burnout; ACD (generative AI) may help.
  - Aim: Assess real-world effects on WOW, documentation delay, and financial productivity; impact remains uncertain (results not reported in abstract).

- Narrative and AI-assisted follow-up after voluntary medical male circumcision (multicenter, double-blind, prospective RCT)
  - Problem: Postoperative anxiety, limited telemedicine access, and suboptimal communication hinder recovery/adherence.
  - Intervention: Narrative-based strategies plus LLM-enabled automated follow-up; role in VMMC care underexplored (results not reported in abstract).

- LLMs for extracting aortic information from abdominal imaging reports (real-world database)
  - Problem: AAA surveillance is resource-intensive; traditional NLP needs task-specific training.
  - Aim: Evaluate generalized LLMs to extract AAA-related data without task-tuned training at scale in real-world settings.

- Guidelines and checklists for AI in surgical education research (narrative review)
  - Contribution: Synthesizes existing AI research guideline
