Tool `Paper Search:search_arxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'foundation models healthcare 2024 survey', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 4699 tokens): - Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (cs.CV)
  - Problem: Video reasoning traces in MLLMs often look convincing but are logically inconsistent or weakly grounded in visuals.
  - Diagnostics: Introduces Think-Answer Consistency (TAC) to measure alignment between reasoning and answers, and Video Attention Score (VAS) to quantify reliance on visual vs. textual cues. Analysis over 11 video reasoning benchmarks shows strong reliance on linguistic priors.
  - Method: Two-stage post-training—timestamp-aware supervised fine-tuning plus GRPO reinforcement learning with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporally aligned, causally coherent reasoning.
  - Results: Consistently higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; argues better temporal alignment/consistency yields more accurate, trustworthy video understanding. Code/data/model to be open-sourced.

- Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (cs.CV)
  - Problem: Most MLLMs “think about videos” in text only after a single encoding; cannot rewatch/refocus/verify, limiting fine-grained spatiotemporal reasoning.
  - Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—“think with videos” by iteratively manipulating visual input.
  - Model: Video-CoM executes a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) to gather/refine evidence.
  - Data/RL: Video-CoM Instruct (18K) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; RL via GRPO with step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
  - Results: Strong performance on 9 video reasoning benchmarks, +3.6% average over recent SOTA while training on only 25K SFT + 3K GRPO samples; improved accuracy and interpretability via reasoning-aware rewards. Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

- Thinking by Doing: Efficient World Model Reasoning via Multi-turn Interaction (cs.AI)
  - Goal: Build robust world model reasoning for LLM agents through efficient interaction and active reasoning, avoiding rigid reasoning pipelines.
  - Framework (WMAct): 
    - Reward rescaling to incentivize effective, non-redundant actions.
    - Interaction frequency annealing to progressively cap turns, encouraging internalized dynamics understanding rather than over-reliance on feedback.
  - Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, WMAct solves tasks in a single turn that previously needed multiple interactions and transfers better to more complex environments, boosting reasoning benchmarks.

- AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (cs.CV)
  - Challenge: Multi-person audio-driven talking video generation is data-expensive and hard to keep identities interactive and coherent.
  - Architecture: Extensible multi-stream Diffusion Transformer with identity-aware attention that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, scaling to many identities.
  - Training: Primarily from single-person videos to learn speaking patterns; refine interactivity with a small number of real multi-person clips.
  - Evaluation: New metric and dataset for naturalness and interactivity. Achieves strong lip-sync, visual quality, and natural multi-person interactivity, balancing data cost and identity scalability.

- ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (cs.LG; cs.CL)
  - Context: Simplifies and extends AlphaEvolve into an open, single-LLM framework that scales in-context learning and RL at test time to evolve programs for improving bounds on open optimization problems.
  - Key components: Large program database, batch sampling for throughput, lazy penalties to discourage stagnation, optional reward shaping for stability.
  - Results: First to enable a small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) to achieve new best-known bounds (circle packing, first auto-correlation inequality). Across two models and four tasks, test-time RL outperforms inference-only baselines; RL-trained checkpoints show faster and better progress on trained and unseen tasks. Code: htt
