Tool `Paper Search:search_medrxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'clinical large language model 2024', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6884 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:
- Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa)
  - Goal: Build and evaluate an LLM specialized for clinical psychiatry using non-English, population-based EHR notes.
  - Data/Model: PsyRoBERTa pretrained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes covering eastern Denmark.
  - Comparators: Three public models (general or medical-domain) and a logistic regression baseline.
  - Outcomes: Predict acute psychiatric readmissions; also tested diagnosis recognition.
  - Results: Outperformed prior models for readmissions (AUC=0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85). Captured clinically aligned features.
  - Implications: Domain-specific pretraining is important; demonstrates potential to leverage psychiatric clinical notes for outcome prediction.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
  - Goal: Estimate superspreading (dispersion r) from early spatial spread using population-level data.
  - Methods: Branching process with US inter-county mobility; secondary infections ~ negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r. Graph neural network inference robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Findings: Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases variability across realizations. Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown r=0.50 (95% CI: 0.23–1.20), weaker post-lockdown r=1.3 (0.64–3.18).
  - Implication: New approach to quantify superspreading from spatial spread patterns without detailed contact/genomic data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland
  - Design: Controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021). Policy: Aberdeen—38 bars extended 1–3 hours to 3am; Glasgow—10 nightclubs 1-hour extension to 4am. Control: Edinburgh.
  - Primary outcome: Weekend night-time alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: crimes.
  - Results (Aberdeen): Significant relative increases—ambulance call-outs +17.4% (effect size=7.061; 95% CI: 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); reported crimes +7.9% (effect size=3.187; 95% CI: 0.564–5.810; p=0.017), though crime finding not robust across sensitivity analyses.
  - Results (Glasgow): Not significant/robust.
  - Conclusion: Later trading hours increased harms in Aberdeen; effects likely moderated by number, capacity, and type of premises. Policy relevance for local/national licensing.

