Tool `Paper Search:search_biorxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'foundation model genomics 2024 review', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 5526 tokens): - Programmed Delayed Splicing: A Mechanism for Timed Inflammatory Gene Expression (immunology; doi:10.1101/443796; 2025-11-24). Using hybrid capture of chromatin-associated, incompletely spliced pre-mRNAs and deep sequencing of the NF-κB transcriptome with transcription “freeze” assays, the authors show many introns splice tens to hundreds of times slower than average. Conserved introns with weak donor sites act as timing elements; strengthening donor sites by as few as two nucleotides in minigene reporters markedly increases expression, indicating delayed splicing both slows mRNA production and limits amplitude. Deep learning sequence-to-function models with feature attribution identify additional intronic/exonic cis-elements and non-canonical motifs enriched in slow introns, supporting temporal control of inflammatory gene expression via programmed splicing delays.

- Ddx3x regulates B-cell development and immunoglobulin rearrangement in mice (immunology; doi:10.1101/452086; 2025-11-28). Conditional Ddx3x loss (Mb1-Cre) in female mouse B cells causes a complete block at the pro-B stage and absence of mature peripheral B cells; hemizygous male deletions (Vav1-Cre or Mb1-Cre) show milder reductions and marginal zone skewing, suggesting partial compensation by Ddx3y or male factors. Mechanistically, Ddx3x loss in pre-B cells reduces histone reader Brwd1 expression, fails to curtail proliferation, and impairs Igκ rearrangement, skewing the light chain repertoire toward λ. Ddx3x is essential for proliferative and epigenetic programs enabling immunoglobulin gene rearrangement.

- Loss of ErbB3 redirects Integrin β1 from early endosomal recycling to secretion in extracellular vesicles (cell biology; doi:10.1101/575449; 2025-11-13). In breast epithelial cells, ErbB3 sorts internalized integrin β1 and transferrin receptor for endocytic recycling independent of ligand signaling. ErbB3 depletion blocks Rab4-compartment recycling, redirects integrin β1 to lysosomal degradation and EV cargo, reduces cell-surface integrin β1, impairs collective migration, and increases release of integrin β1–containing EVs that paradoxically enhance motility of wild-type cells. ErbB3 promotes assembly of an Arf6–GGA3–Rabaptin5 sorting complex, facilitating early recycling and suppressing EV release, revealing a trafficking role for pseudo-RTKs.

- Supercentenarian and remarkable age records exhibit patterns indicative of clerical errors and pension fraud (developmental biology; doi:10.1101/704080; 2025-11-03). In the U.S., supercentenarian counts drop 69–82% after state-level introduction of birth certificates, indicating dependence on absent vital registration. In Italy, England, and France with uniform registration, remarkable ages correlate with poverty, low income, shorter life expectancy, higher crime, worse health, higher deprivation, fewer 90+ residents, and remote/overseas locales; old-age poverty explains >50% of regional variation in England/France. Only 18% of validated supercentenarians have birth certificates (0% in the U.S.), with birthdates clustered on days divisible by five. “Blue zones” coincide with low income/literacy, high crime, and short lifespan, supporting fraud/error as primary drivers of extreme age records.

- Oscillatory Multi-Timescale Mechanisms Underlying Audiovisual Sequence Prediction (neuroscience; doi:10.1101/778969; 2025-11-05). MEG during an audiovisual serial prediction task (visual or auditory features task-relevant) and data-driven clustering reveal theta-band (5–7 Hz) coupling across cingulate, premotor, prefrontal, and superior temporal regions; behavioral performance varies with phase delays between nodes. Two beta-power clusters (11–16 Hz, 16–22 Hz) increase when preceding or following items match the current sequence element. Theta and beta clusters spatially overlap and interact via cross-frequency coupling, indicating multi-timescale oscillatory coordination supports multisensory sequence prediction.

