Overall
- Nine studies examine large language models (LLMs)/NLP across nutrition, clinical documentation, oncology, radiology safety, medical education, cohort identification, traditional Chinese medicine (TCM), and multidisciplinary treatment (MDT). Most provide early, feasibility-focused evidence; several lack detailed outcome metrics in abstracts.

1) Language Models in Nutrition and Dietetics: A Scoping Review (O’Hara et al.)
- Scope: Maps how LMs are applied in nutrition/dietetics, highlighting opportunities and risks (inaccuracy, bias, data protection).
- Methods: Pre-registered scoping review across Medline, EMBASE, PsycINFO, CINAHL, PubMed, Global Health, Web of Science, Scopus, DBLP, IEEE Xplore, plus citation chasing.
- Results: 2,118 records screened; 40 studies included (published 2022–2024). Twenty-three different LMs used; 9 studies built domain-specific models via retrieval-augmented generation or fine-tuning. Seventeen nutrition-related diseases addressed.
- Tasks: Evidence extraction/summarization from literature or EHRs (n=5), food composition estimation (n=9), meal plan creation (n=7), dietary advice (n=21).
- Evidence quality: 38 studies evaluated models in hypothetical settings (e.g., designed case studies/questions), with limited real-world implementations or domain-specific tools.
- Conclusion: Emerging area with sparse real-world evidence. Calls for implementation studies and inclusion of nutritionist/dietitian, patient, and public perspectives.

2) Shifts in Emergency Physicians’ Attitudes Toward LLM-Based Documentation (Lee et al.)
- Setting: Emergency department discharge note drafting with an LLM assistant.
- Participants: 8 emergency physicians (mean experience 12 years).
- Design: Surveys pre-use, after 3 days, and after 5 weeks of LLM use.
- Findings: Concerns about LLMs fell significantly and stayed low. Perceived workload dropped; time per discharge note decreased by ~one-third. Indicates rapid acceptance and sustained perceived benefit, offering implementation insights for LLM documentation tools.

3) Hybrid Ambient Clinical Documentation and Physician Performance (Moura et al.)
- Focus: Real-world impact of generative AI-powered ambient clinical documentation (ACD) on clinician burden and productivity.
- Outcomes of interest: After-hours EHR “work outside of work” (WOW), documentation delay, and financial productivity.
- Rationale: WOW contributes to burnout; ACD may help, but real-world effects are uncertain. Study targets these operational endpoints.

4) Integrating a Shareable AI Model for Cancer Recurrence From Radiology Reports (Cao et al.)
- Tool: DFCI “imaging-student” NLP model that extracts cancer outcomes from radiology reports.
- Implementation: Applied in a community oncology setting; aggregated report-level predictions to patient-level outcomes.
- Objectives: Determine recurrence and time-to-recurrence in breast cancer and colorectal cancer.
- Contribution: Demonstrates portability/shareability of a recurrence-extraction NLP model beyond its original academic setting.

5) Detecting Laterality Errors in Combined Radiographic Studies With GPT-4o (Weng et al.)
- Problem: Laterality errors in radiology reports (especially combined studies) threaten patient safety; screening methods are underdeveloped for combined reports.
- Approach: Enhances traditional detection with GPT-4o; algorithm development and multisite internal validation.
- Aim: Improve safety by more effectively flagging laterality inconsistencies.

