d from 37.3 (38th percentile) to 30 (14th percentile) among control providers. Experiencing burnout dropped from 8 (89%) to 5 (56%) among intervention providers but remained stable at 3 (38%) in the control group. There was no significant change to pajama time (-9.4 minutes per scheduled day, 95% CI: -41.2 to +22.4), time in notes per encounter (+0.2 minutes per note, 95% CI: -6.6 to +6.9), or work Relative Value Units (wRVUs) per encounter (-0.03, 95% CI: -0.5 to +0.44). Of 48 qualitative comments, 69% had a positive sentiment, 15% neutral, and 17% negative.Among pediatric subspecialists, AI scribes improved clinician experience and burnout without changing charting time or EHR work outside work hours.","doi":"10.1055/a-2657-8087","published_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40675605/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""},{"paper_id":"40672471","title":"A Randomized-Clinical Trial of Two Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes: Measuring Documentation Efficiency and Physician Burnout.","authors":"Lukac PJ; Turner W; Vangala S; Chin AT; Khalili J; Shih YT; Sarkisian C; Cheng EM; Mafi JN","abstract":"Ambient artificial intelligence (AI) scribes record patient encounters and generate visit notes almost instantaneously, representing a promising solution to documentation burden and associated physician burnout. Despite swift and widespread adoption of AI scribes, their impacts have not been examined in randomized-clinical trials.","doi":"10.1101/2025.07.10.25331333","published_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","pdf_url":"","url":"https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40672471/","source":"pubmed","updated_date":"2025-01-01T00:00:00","categories":"","keywords":"","citations":0,"references":"","extra":""}]
