1) Video-R2: Reinforcing Consistent and Grounded Reasoning in Multimodal Language Models (Maaz et al., cs.CV, arXiv:2511.23478)
- Problem: Video reasoning in MLLMs often sounds convincing but lacks logical consistency and grounding in visuals.
- Diagnostics: Introduces Think Answer Consistency (TAC) for reasoning–answer alignment and Video Attention Score (VAS) for visual vs. textual dependency. Analysis across 11 video benchmarks shows over-reliance on linguistic priors.
- Method: Two-stage post-training: timestamp-aware SFT + GRPO with a new Temporal Alignment Reward (TAR) to enforce temporal precision and causal coherence.
- Results: Higher TAC, VAS, and accuracy across multiple benchmarks; more accurate and trustworthy video reasoning.
- Release: Code, dataset, and model to be open-sourced.

2) Video-CoM: Interactive Video Reasoning via Chain of Manipulations (Rasheed et al., cs.CV, arXiv:2511.23477)
- Problem: Most MLLMs “think about” videos passively after one encoding; they cannot rewatch/refocus/verify, limiting fine-grained spatio-temporal reasoning.
- Paradigm: Interactive Video Reasoning—“think with videos” via a Chain of Manipulations (CoM) that iteratively performs visual actions to gather/refine evidence.
- Training: Video-CoM Instruct (18K instructions) for multi-step manipulation reasoning; RL with reasoning-aware GRPO using step-level reasoning rewards (beyond sparse answer rewards).
- Results: Strong results on 9 video reasoning benchmarks; +3.6% average over recent SOTA while using only 25K SFT and 3K GRPO video samples. Improved accuracy and interpretability in ablations.
- Code: https://github.com/mbzuai-oryx/Video-CoM

3) Thinking by Doing: Building Efficient World Model Reasoning in LLMs via Multi-turn Interaction (Shu et al., cs.AI, arXiv:2511.23476)
- Problem: Rigid reasoning pipelines hinder efficient world model internalization during multi-turn interactions.
- Approach (WMAct): Lets models learn by “doing,” with:
  - Reward rescaling based on action efficacy to reduce redundancy and promote purposeful interactions.
  - Interaction frequency annealing to progressively limit max turns, encouraging internalization of dynamics.
- Results: On Sokoban, Maze, Taxi, WMAct achieves single-turn solutions where multiple were previously needed, shows strong transfer, and improves broader reasoning benchmarks.

4) AnyTalker: Scaling Multi-Person Talking Video Generation with Interactivity Refinement (Zhong et al., cs.CV, arXiv:2511.23475)
- Problem: Multi-person audio-driven talking video generation is limited by data costs and achieving coherent interactivity across identities.
- Method: Extensible multi-stream architecture with identity-aware attention in a Diffusion Transformer that iteratively processes identity–audio pairs, enabling arbitrary identity scaling.
- Training: Uses only single-person videos to learn multi-person speaking patterns; refines interactivity with a small set of real multi-person clips.
- Contributions: New metric and dataset to evaluate naturalness and interactivity.
- Results: Strong lip sync, visual quality, and natural interactivity; balances data cost and scalability.

5) ThetaEvolve: Test-time Learning on Open Problems (Wang et al., cs.LG/cs.CL, arXiv:2511.23473)
- Goal: Open-source, simplified, and extended AlphaEvolve-like system enabling test-time in-context learning + RL to solve open optimization problems with a single LLM.
- Features: Large program database, batch sampling, lazy penalties to discourage stagnation, optional reward shaping; models continually learn evolving strategies.
- Results: Small open-source model (DeepSeek-R1-0528-Qwen3-8B) achieves new best-known bounds on circle packing and first auto-correlation inequality. Across two models/four tasks, RL at test-time > inference-only; RL checkpoints learn to progress faster and generalize to unseen tasks.
