  - Design/aim: Systematic review and meta-analysis comparing one-time FIT vs one-time colonoscopy for screening uptake and diagnostic yield of CRC, non-advanced adenomas, and advanced adenomas.
  - Context: CRC remains a leading cause of morbidity/mortality; incidence rising in younger populations.
  - Key focus: Balance between higher screening uptake (typically seen with non-invasive tests) versus diagnostic yield (often higher with colonoscopy).

- Artificial intelligence for reducing missed detection of adenomas and polyps in colonoscopy: A systematic review and meta-analysis (Wang et al., 2025; doi:10.3748/wjg.v31.i21.105753; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40538513/)
  - Design/aim: Systematic review/meta-analysis of CADe systems integrated into colonoscopy to reduce missed lesions.
  - Key emphasis: Adenoma miss rate (AMR) as a more sensitive endpoint than adenoma detection rate (ADR); seeks to quantify CADe’s impact on missed detection of adenomas and polyps.

- Computer-aided diagnosis for colorectal polyp in comparison with endoscopists: Systematic review and meta-analysis (Shinozaki et al., 2025; doi:10.1111/den.15047; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/40375757/)
  - Design/aim: Compares diagnostic accuracy of CADx for real-time optical diagnosis of colorectal polyps vs experienced and inexperienced endoscopists.
  - Focus: Histology prediction performance and potential to standardize/improve optical diagnosis across experience levels.

