Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'foundation models healthcare 2024 review', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2479 tokens): - Predicting human tactile smoothness/roughness perception from multidimensional mechanical properties of synthetic fibers using machine learning (DOI: 10.1038/s41598-025-26294-5; 2025-01-01)
  - Goal: Improve prediction of human tactile roughness perception by integrating multidimensional mechanical properties with subjective evaluations.
  - Data: 50 commercial synthetic fiber samples (polyester, spandex, nylon, blends).
  - Measures: 12 mechanical properties in four categories—geometric roughness, frictional force, hardness, tensile strength.
  - Human ratings: 37 participants; 5-point scale (lower=smoother, higher=rougher).
  - Key predictors (Spearman ρ): kinetic friction coefficient KF (-0.67), arithmetic mean roughness Ra (0.44), mean width of profile elements RSm (0.42), maximum load ML (-0.41), root mean square slope Rdq (0.31).
  - Modeling: Six regression models compared; Gaussian process regression had the highest cross-validated predictive accuracy (value not specified in abstract).
  - Significance: Addresses prior limits of narrow property sets, small samples, and weak validation by combining multidimensional mechanics with perception.

- Accelerating eSource scale-up in oncology clinical trials: The i~HD Task Force initiative (DOI: 10.1017/pcm.2025.10004; 2025-01-01)
  - Context: eSource (EHR-to-EDC) can reduce site burden, improve data quality, and speed oncology trial workflows; adoption hampered by technical, regulatory, and operational barriers.
  - Initiative: 2024 European Institute for Innovation through Health Data (i~HD) eSource Scale-Up Task Force; multi-stakeholder (oncology centers and pharma sponsors).
  - Outputs: Readiness criteria for early adopters; performance indicator framework; operational playbook for implementation.
  - Focus: Interoperability, regulatory alignment, real-world validation.
  - Applicability: Framework generalizable to other data-intensive therapeutic areas.

