- Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa; doi:10.1101/2025.03.07.25323558)
  - Built PsyRoBERTa, the first psychiatry-specialized LLM trained on ~44 million Danish psychiatric-relevant clinical notes (population-based, eastern Denmark).
  - Compared to three public general/medical-domain models and a logistic regression baseline.
  - Task: predict acute psychiatric readmissions; also assessed diagnosis recognition and feature importance.
  - Results: PsyRoBERTa outperformed prior models (AUC=0.74) and recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85).
  - Implication: Domain-specific pretraining substantially improves leveraging psychiatric notes for outcome prediction, including non-English data.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns (doi:10.1101/2025.10.28.25338965)
  - Modeled US inter-county spatial spread via branching process with negative binomial transmission (mean R0, dispersion r quantifying superspreading).
  - Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases growth variability across realizations.
  - A graph neural network inferred r from early spatial spread; robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - COVID-19 application (US): strong superspreading pre-lockdown r=0.50 (95% CI: 0.23–1.20) vs weaker post-lockdown r=1.3 (0.64–3.18).
  - Provides a population-level approach to quantify superspreading without cluster or genomic data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland (doi:10.1101/2025.10.30.25339133)
  - Natural experiment with controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021).
  - Aberdeen: 38 bars extended by 1–3 hours (to 3am). Glasgow: 10 nightclubs extended by 1 hour (to 4am). Edinburgh served as control.
  - Primary outcome: weekend nighttime alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: crimes.
  - Aberdeen: significant relative increase in call-outs +17.4% (effect size=7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001); crimes +7.9% (effect size=3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses). Glasgow: no significant robust effects.
  - Implication: Later hours can increase harms, moderated by number/capacity/type of premises; informs licensing policy design.

- Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume: Age Attenuates Protection in Females (doi:10.1101/2025.10.30.25339193)
