1) Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR (PsyRoBERTa)
- DOI: 10.1101/2025.03.07.25323558 | Category: Health informatics | Source: medRxiv
- Data/model: First psychiatry-specialized LLM pretrained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark (non-English). Compared to 3 public models (general/medical-domain) and a logistic regression baseline.
- Task/performance: Predict acute psychiatric readmissions; PsyRoBERTa achieved AUC = 0.74 (best among comparators). Also recognized psychiatric diagnoses (AUC = 0.85).
- Takeaway: Domain-specific pretraining materially improves leveraging psychiatric notes for outcome prediction; model captured clinically aligned features.

2) Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
- DOI: 10.1101/2025.10.28.25338965 | Category: Infectious diseases | Source: medRxiv
- Methods: Branching process with inter-county mobility (US); negative binomial secondary cases with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading). Graph neural network infers r from early spatial spread; robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
- Findings: Stronger superspreading slows early geographic invasion and increases variability. Early COVID-19 in US: pre-lockdown r = 0.50 (95% CI 0.23–1.20); post-lockdown r = 1.3 (0.64–3.18).
- Takeaway: Early spatial spread patterns can identify superspreading potential from population-level data without intensive contact tracing/genomics.

3) The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland
- DOI: 10.1101/2025.10.30.25339133 | Category: Public and global health | Source: medRxiv
- Design: Controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021). Aberdeen: 38 bars extended 1–3 hours up to 3 a.m.; Glasgow: 10 nightclubs extended 1 hour to 4 a.m.; controls included Edinburgh. Outcomes: weekend nighttime alcohol-related ambulance call-outs (primary), crimes (secondary).
- Results: Aberdeen saw a 17.4% relative increase in ambulance call-outs (effect size 7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p < 0.001) and 7.9% increase in reported crimes (effect size 3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p = 0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses). Glasgow effects were not significant/robust.
- Conclusion: Later hours increased harms where more/larger premises extended hours; local context (number/capacity/type of venue) moderates impacts. Policy relevance for licensing strategies.

4) Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume: Age Attenuates Protection in Females
- DOI: 10.1101/2025.10.30.25339193 | Category: Neurology | Source: medRxiv
- Cohort: AIS registry (2015–2024), n = 1,103; 48% female; median age 66; median NIHSS 9; median DWI-FIV 7.8 cm3.
- Measures: Parenchymal brain volume (PBV) from NCCT; final infarct volume (DWI-FIV) from 24–48 h MRI; outcome mRS 0–2 at 90 days.
- Results: Univariable: greater PBV increased odds of functional independence in both sexes. Multivariable: protective PBV association persisted in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 95% CI 1.10–1.60) but not females (aOR 1.10; 95% CI 0.90–1.33). Each decade of age reduced independence more in females (aOR 0.70; 95% CI 0.62–0.79) vs males (aOR 0.85; 95% CI 0.72–0.97; interaction p = 0.019). Age linked to larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p = 0.033), not males.
- Conclusion: Brain reserve (PBV) is less protective in women as age increases; aging amplifies infarct size/severity in females, attenuating benefit.

5) Concordance between IDSA Criteria for UTI Testing/Treating vs ED Practice
