Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Nature Medicine 2024 artificial intelligence healthcare review', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 2580 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:
Overall: These six 2025 publications span applied AI/ML in medicine (AutoML adoption, real-time AI in endoscopy, AI-enabled coronary CTA guidance, AI for early pregnancy loss prediction), ethical considerations of LLM-simplified radiology reports, and the role of clinical data warehouses.

1) Automated Machine Learning in medical research: A systematic literature mapping study.
- Scope: Systematic literature mapping across multiple academic databases; 244 studies (2016–2025) from 171 sources.
- Use cases: Diagnosis prediction 52.8%, prognosis prediction 31.9%.
- Data types: Tabular 43.4%, image 31.5%.
- Tasks: Classification 81.1%, regression 9.4%.
- Challenges: For non-managed AutoML, model selection was most common challenge (25.3%); data preprocessing remained critical (13.7%) even with managed services.
- XAI: Used in 30.7% overall, with notable uptake increase in 2024.
- Conclusions: AutoML adoption in medicine is growing, but black-box concerns limit use in interpretability-critical domains. Integrating XAI with AutoML is an emerging trend; more research is needed to assess clinical impact and build trust in AutoML decision support.
- DOI: 10.1016/j.artmed.2025.103302

2) AI-assisted colonoscopy and adenoma detection
- Design: Single-center, retrospective, propensity score-matched study with concurrent controls.
- Objective: Assess whether a real-time AI-assisted polyp detection system improves adenoma detection rates (ADRs) in routine, real-world colonoscopy practice.
- Results: Not detailed in abstract (focus on study aim and design).
- DOI: 10.1186/s12876-025-04011-w

3) Ethics of simplification in AI-generated radiology reports
- Context: LLMs (e.g., GPT-4) increasingly simplify radiology reports for patient comprehension.
- Concern: Excessive simplification may compromise clinical accuracy, undermining informed consent and patient autonomy.
- Aim: Identify readability thresholds (minimum reading level) at which clinical accuracy is preserved, balancing comprehension with fidelity.
- Implication: Establishes an ethical framework for readability targets in AI-generated patient-facing medical communications.
- DOI: 10.1186/s12910-025-01285-3

4) Coronary CT angiography (CCTA) with AI for individualized atherosclerosis treatment: QCI Study Group Consensus Statement
- Context: ~2.2 million CCTAs annually for stable chest pain in Europe; AI/ML can improve plaque evaluation reliability and speed.
- Process: Three-step Delphi after the 3rd international QCI Study Group meeting (Sept 2024); experts across cardiovascular imaging.
- Data foundation: Age- and gender-adjusted percentile curves based on plaque data from DISCHARGE and SCOT-HEART trials.
- Key recommendations:
  - Any detectable atherosclerotic plaque should prompt a recommendation for pharmacological treatment.
  - Total plaque volume at or above the 70th percentile warrants high-intensity treatment.
- Goals: Harness AI/ML precision for individualized plaque-based treatment; provide a framework for future trials; improve outcomes globally.
- DOI: 10.1038/s41569-025-01191-6

5) AI to predict early pregnancy loss (EPL) from embryo ultrasound videos
- Data: 630 ultrasound videos from singleton pregnancies (6–10 weeks gestation).
- Approach: Two-stage model:
  - A3F-net (modified U-Net) automatically extracts biometric features: gestational sac area (GSA), yolk sac diameter (YSD), crown-rump length (CRL), and fetal heart rate (FHR).
  - Ensemble learning predicts EPL risk; CatBoost performed best.
- Measurement performance (A3F-net vs other models): Precision—GSA 98.64%, YSD 96.94%, CRL 92.83%.
- Agreement with clinicians: Bland-Altman showed no systematic deviation; mean relative error between doctors and AI 0.060 ± 0.057.
- Prediction performance: CatBoost precision 98.0%; AUC 0.969 (95% CI: 0.962–0.975).
- Conclusion: Hybrid pipeline achieves consistent measurements and strong EPL prediction, supporting timely, accurate clinical decisions.
- DOI
Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'Lancet Digital Health 2024 artificial intelligence review', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1737 tokens): Cross-cutting summary
Across these papers, AI is advancing clinical accuracy, safety, and personalization (infectious disease diagnostics, radiation therapy QA, and medical digital twins), but real-world impact hinges on robust data infrastructure, interoperability, governance, and health system readiness. Blending AI with mechanistic models and deploying rigorous quality assurance tools show promise, while policymakers must align investments with local capacity to avoid widening inequities.

Paper summaries

1) Artificial intelligence and infectious disease diagnostics: state of the art and future perspectives (Miglietta et al.; The Lancet Infectious Diseases; doi:10.1016/S1473-3099(25)00354-8; 2025-01-01)
- Contribution: Reviews how AI supports clinical decision-making, optimizes lab/clinical workflows, and enables real-time disease surveillance. Demonstrates improvements in pathogen detection, antimicrobial stewardship, and treatment monitoring—enhancing diagnostic accuracy, efficiency, and scalability.
- AMR impact: Highlights AI’s role in combating antimicrobial resistance via rapid pathogen identification and personalized therapy selection.
- Barriers:
  - High-income countries: fragmented data ecosystems, incomplete datasets, algorithmic bias impede integration.
  - Low- and middle-income countries: limited digital infrastructure, unstandardized data, and financial constraints exacerbate diagnostic inequities.
  - Cross-cutting: interoperability, data privacy, cybersecurity, and regulatory challenges.
- Recommendations: Coordinated investments in digital infrastructure, harmonized data-sharing frameworks, and clinician engagement to achieve equitable and sustainable adoption; leveraging AI to strengthen global health resilience.

2) Medical digital twins: enabling precision medicine and medical artificial intelligence (Sadée et al.; Lancet Digital Health; doi:10.1016/j.landig.2025.02.004; 2025-01-01)
- Concept: Adapts engineering digital twins (continuously updating virtual replicas) to medicine.
- Framework: Defines five components—patient, data connection, patient-in-silico (computational model), interface, and twin synchronization.
- Enablers: Multimodal data integration, AI, and mechanistic modeling; emphasizes data fusion and combining AI with mechanistic models to offset each approach’s limitations.
- Applications: Examples in oncology and diabetes; outlines how digital twins can bolster large language model performance in clinical contexts.
- Goal: Guidance for scientists, clinicians, and policymakers to translate digital twins from concept to clinical practice.

3) Building health systems capable of leveraging AI: applying Paul Farmer’s 5S framework for equitable global health (McCoy et al.; Globalization and Health; doi:10.1186/s44263-025-00158-6; 2025-01-01)
- Thesis: The primary barrier to equitable AI in global health is not just algorithmic bias but the mismatch between AI’s promised benefits and the material realities of under-resourced health systems.
- Risks: Investing in AI without adequate social/material infrastructure can misallocate scarce resources and cause harm.
- Contribution: Calls for assessing systemic capacity and socio-technical readiness (guided by Farmer’s equity-centric principles) before AI deployment; provides concrete questions to evaluate readiness across services, staff, systems, and resources.

4) A prospectively deployed deep learning-enabled automated quality assurance tool for oncological palliative spine radiation therapy (Kehayias et al.; The Lancet Digital Health; doi:10.1016/S2589-7500(24)00243-7; 2025-01-01)
- Innovation: DL-SpiQA, a fully automated deep learning system for spine-targeting quality assurance that detects treatment at the wrong anatomical level in palliative spine radiotherapy.
- Validation and deployment: Assessed via retrospective testing and implemented prospectively in clinical workflow, aiming to reduce wrong-level treatments and improve patient safety.

