Key studies

1) Developing and validating a T2DM risk model after GDM (BMJ Open, 2025; protocol)
- Population/context: Women with prior gestational diabetes mellitus (GDM), who have a 7–10× higher risk of progressing to type 2 diabetes mellitus (T2DM), with risk increasing over time post-partum.
- Aim: Develop and validate a model to predict 5-year and 10-year T2DM risk after a GDM pregnancy.
- Design: Population-based data linkage study and protocol outlining model development/validation.
- Expected impact: Risk stratification tool to guide follow-up and preventive care post-GDM.

2) Predicting patient outcomes and revision risk after THA/TKA using SIRIS (Study protocol)
- Population/context: Patients undergoing total hip (THA) or total knee arthroplasty (TKA); outcomes include patient-reported outcomes (PROs) and risk of revision surgery.
- Aim: Compare predictive performance of traditional logistic regression (LR) versus multiple machine learning (ML) methods.
- Data source: Swiss National Joint Registry (SIRIS).
- Focus: Head-to-head methodological comparison to clarify if ML offers performance advantages over LR for postoperative prediction.
- Expected impact: Evidence to inform method choice for clinical decision support, resource allocation, and care planning in arthroplasty.

3) Systematic review of ML-based prognostic models for acute pancreatitis (PLOS Medicine, 2025)
- Problem: Lack of robust, clinically useful prognostic tools for acute pancreatitis (AP) despite increasing ML-based model development.
- Aim: Critically appraise methodological and reporting quality of recent ML prognostic models in AP.
- Approach: Collaboration with ML methodology experts; focus on validity, reproducibility, and factors affecting clinical implementation.
- Expected impact: Identify gaps and best practices to improve model quality, transparency, and readiness for clinical use.

4) Plasma metabolomics predicts prediabetes in chronic pancreatitis before HbA1c (Clin Transl Gastroenterol, 2024)
- Population/context: Nondiabetic patients with chronic pancreatitis (CP) at risk of pancreatogenic diabetes (T3cDM) due to endocrine/metabolic dysfunction.
- Aim: Use plasma metabolite profiling to detect early glycemic alterations and identify individuals progressing from normoglycemia toward hyperglycemia before HbA1c changes.
- Key finding: Metabolic alterations measurable in plasma can flag progression to prediabetes earlier than conventional HbA1c, enabling earlier intervention.
- Implication: A potential biomarker-based strategy for early detection and prevention of T3cDM in CP.

5) APPRAISE-AI: Quantitative tool to evaluate AI studies for clinical decision support (JAMA Netw Open, 2023)
- Problem: Existing AI reporting guidelines lack a way to quantify overall research quality, hindering comparison of models addressing the same clinical question.
- Aim: Provide a standardized, quantitative evaluation tool (APPRAISE-AI) to assess methodological rigor and fairness in AI studies.
- Value: Enables objective comparison and prioritization of AI models for clinical deployment; complements reporting guidelines with a quality score.

6) Peripheral blood transcriptomic signature to predict bronchopulmonary dysplasia (AJP Lung, 2023)
