Tool `Paper Search:search_medrxiv` with Parameter {'query': 'Randomized-Clinical Trial of Two Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes Lukac', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 6884 tokens): - Evaluating large language models for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions from clinical notes of population-based EHR
  - Introduces PsyRoBERTa, an LLM specialized for clinical psychiatry, pretrained on ~44 million psychiatric-relevant clinical notes from eastern Denmark (non-English).
  - Outperformed three public general/medical-domain models and a logistic regression baseline for predicting psychiatric acute readmissions (AUC=0.74) and recognizing psychiatric diagnoses (AUC=0.85).
  - Captured clinically aligned features; highlights importance of domain-specific pretraining and the promise of leveraging unstructured psychiatric notes to predict outcomes.

- Inferring pathogen superspreading potential using early spatial spread patterns
  - Uses a branching process with US inter-county mobility; individual transmission modeled via negative binomial with mean R0 and dispersion r (superspreading).
  - Stronger superspreading slows early spatial invasion and increases realization-to-realization variability.
  - A graph neural network infers r from early spatial spread, robust to spatiotemporal R0 variation and underreporting.
  - Early US COVID-19: strong superspreading pre-lockdown (r=0.50, 95% CI 0.23–1.20), weaker after (r=1.3, 0.64–3.18).
  - Offers a population-level approach to quantify superspreading without cluster/genomic data.

- The impact of later trading hours for bars and clubs on alcohol-related ambulance call-outs and crimes in Scotland
  - Controlled interrupted time series (2015–2021) of local policy changes: Aberdeen (38 bars +1–3 h to 3am), Glasgow (10 nightclubs +1 h to 4am); Edinburgh as control.
  - Primary outcome: weekend night-time alcohol-related ambulance call-outs; secondary: crimes.
  - Aberdeen: significant relative increases in ambulance call-outs (+17.4%; effect size=7.061; 95% CI 3.902–10.220; p<0.001) and crimes (+7.9%; effect size=3.187; 95% CI 0.564–5.810; p=0.017; not robust in sensitivity analyses).
  - Glasgow: no significant, robust effects.
  - Implication: impacts depend on number/capacity/type of premises; informs national/local licensing policy design.

- Sex Differences in the Protective Effect of Brain Volume: Age Attenuates Protection in Females
  - Multicenter AIS registry (2015–2024; n=1,103; 48% female). PBV from non-contrast CT; infarct volume (DWI-FIV) from 24–48h MRI. Outcome: 90-day mRS 0–2.
  - Univariable: higher PBV associated with independence in both sexes.
  - Multivariable: protective effect persists in males (aOR 1.30 per 100 cm3; 95% CI 1.10–1.60), not females (aOR 1.10; 0.90–1.33).
  - Each decade of age reduces odds of independence by 30% in females (aOR 0.70; 0.62–0.79) vs 15% in males (aOR 0.85; 0.72–0.97; interaction p=0.019). Age linked to larger infarcts in females (+4.22 cm3/decade; p=0.033).
  - Conclusion: Aging diminishes brain reserve in women; include sex and PBV in outcome models.

- Concordance between IDSA criteria for UTI testing/treating vs ED practice
  - 15-month review of 50 adult ED patients receiving urinalysis (excluding sepsis/empiric antibiotics) to assess adherence to IDSA guidance (avoid testing/treating asymptomatic bacteriuria except specific cases).
  - 64% asymptomatic; none met IDSA criteria for UA.
  - Symptomatic patients more likely UA-positive (72.2% vs 43.8%; p=0.06), more often prescribed antibiotics when UA positive (61.5% vs 14.3%; p=0.0128) and even when UA negative (20.0% vs 0%; p=0.05).
  - Highlights significant discordance; underscores need for educational interventions to reduce unnecessary testing and antibiotic use.

- Mean corpuscular volume in HFE p.C282Y/p.H63D compound heterozygotes with high iron phenotypes
