Tool `Paper Search:read_medrxiv_paper` with Parameter {'paper_id': '10.1101/2025.07.10.25331333', 'save_path': './downloads'} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 15614 tokens): Title: A Randomized Clinical Trial of Two Ambient Artificial Intelligence Scribes: Measuring Documentation Efficiency and Physician Burnout (medRxiv preprint; posted July 11, 2025; CC BY-NC-ND 4.0; not peer reviewed). Trial registration: NCT06792890.

Objective
Evaluate the effectiveness and safety of two ambient AI scribe applications (Microsoft DAX and Nabla) on documentation time and physician burnout.

Design, Setting, Participants
- Parallel, three-arm, pragmatic randomized clinical trial using covariate-constrained randomization (balanced on baseline time-in-note, baseline burnout, clinic days/week).
- Conducted 11/4/2024–1/3/2025 at a large academic health system (UCLA Health).
- 313 recruited; 238 outpatient physicians across 14 specialties enrolled and randomized: DAX (N=79), Nabla (N=79), Control (N=80).
- Inclusion: ≥1 half-day clinic/week; intervention-arm physicians using human scribes discontinued them during the study; English-only visits due to unvalidated translation.
- IRB determination: not human subjects research. Protocol aligned with SPIRIT-AI and CONSORT-AI; rapid deployment precluded timely preregistration; protocol submitted 12/9/2024, published 1/27/2025.

Interventions
- DAX v2.0 and Nabla v1.5 integrated into Epic; AI-generated text populated native note types.
- Standardized training: 1-hour session pre-study; advanced features session two weeks after onset; ongoing internal and vendor support; discretionary discontinuation allowed.
- California two-party consent: verbal consent obtained and documented for recordings.

Outcomes
- Primary: change from baseline in log time-in-note (Epic Signal), comparing second intervention month (12/2/24–1/3/25) to the prior six months (one-month proficiency lead-in).
- Secondary: Mini-Z 2.0 (10–50; higher better), 4-item physician Task Load (0–400; lower better), Professional Fulfillment Index–Work Exhaustion (PFI-WE; 0–4; lower better); EHR time on unscheduled days (Signal); post-study Likert items on usability, accuracy, bias, and safety.
- Sample size/power: ≥79 per arm gave 80% power to detect 0.5 SD at α=0.025 (Bonferroni). Log-scale SD≈0.31; baseline geometric mean time-in-note≈4m43s; detectable effect≈15.5% (~44s).

Statistical Analysis
- Intention-to-treat. Linear mixed-effects model for log time-in-note with arm, period, and interaction; primary contrasts vs control in month 2. Linear regression for survey changes; logistic regression for dichotomized Mini-Z thresholds. Post hoc: association of scribe usage rate with outcomes. α=0.025 (primary); α=0.05 otherwise.

Use and Exposure
- DAX used in 8,271/24,696 visits (33.5%); Nabla in 6,981/23,653 (29.5%); control encounters=24,020.
- ~15% of intervention physicians never used their assigned tool.

Primary Outcome (Time-in-note)
- Estimated declines: Control −18s (4:22→4:04); DAX −23s (4:29→4:06); Nabla −41s (4:30→3:49).
- Nabla vs Control: −9.5% (95% CI −17.2%, −1.8%); p=.02.
- DAX vs Control: −1.7% (−9.4%, +5.9%); p=.66 (not significant).
- Nabla vs DAX: −7.8% (−15.5%, −0.1%); p=.05.
- Higher usage correlated with greater time savings (r: DAX −0.29; Nabla −0.41).

Secondary Outcomes
- Mini-Z total (any scribe vs Control): +2.76 (95% CI +1.41, +4.10); p<.001.
  - Subscales: Supportive Work Environment +1.53 (+0.69, +2.37); p<.001. Work Pace & EMR Stress +1.30 (+0.41, +2.20); p=.004.
- Task Load (any scribe vs Control): −35.79 (−63.71, −7.87); p=.01.
- PFI-WE (any scribe vs Control): −0.27 (−0.48, −0.07); p=.01.
- No significant differences between DAX and Nabla on psychometrics.
- Usage correlated with improved Mini-Z and PFI-WE for Nabla (r=+0.29; r=−0.22) more than DAX (r=+0.03; r=−0.10).
- EHR time on unscheduled days: no significant differences.

Accuracy, Bias, Safety, Usability
- Inaccuracies “occasionally”: DAX 2.7 (2.4–3.0) vs Nabla 2.8 (2.6–3.0); p=.68.
- Bias “rarely”: DAX 1.6 (1.4–1.8) vs Nabla 1.7 (1.5–1.9); p=.38.
- Common issues: omissions (n=12), structural/formatting/verbosity concerns (n=11
