1) Full Guideline
- Citation: 2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol. DOI: 10.1161/CIR.0000000000000625. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30586774/ (Published 2019-01-01).
- Purpose and scope: Translates scientific evidence into clinical practice recommendations to improve cardiovascular health and guide management of blood cholesterol in patients with or at risk for cardiovascular disease (CVD). Focused on U.S. practice but globally relevant.
- Intended use: Provides guideline-directed management and therapy (evaluation, diagnostic testing, pharmacologic and procedural treatments). Not a substitute for clinical judgment; may inform but is not intended for regulatory or payer decisions. Emphasizes shared decision-making to improve adherence, incorporating patient values, preferences, comorbidities, and conditions.
- Development and sponsorship: Sponsored by ACC and AHA without commercial support; writing and review by volunteer members. Recommendations based on systematic methods to evaluate and classify evidence; intended to support quality cardiovascular care.
- Committee composition and inclusivity: Writing committee selected to ensure requisite expertise and broad representation across geography, sex, race/ethnicity, perspectives, and clinical practice scopes. Related societies invited as partners/collaborators; participating groups include AACVPR, AAPA, ABC, ACPM, ADA, AGS, APhA, ASPC, NLA, and PCNA.
- Conflict of interest and transparency: ACC/AHA maintain rigorous policies on relationships with industry and other entities (RWI), available online; processes designed to prevent bias and improper influence.
- Usability and format updates (initiated in 2017): 
  - Guidelines shortened and made more user-friendly.
  - Organized into modular “knowledge chunks,” each with a table of recommendations, brief synopsis, recommendation-specific supportive text, and when appropriate, flow diagrams or additional tables.
  - Hyperlinked references facilitate quick access.
  - Word limits (“targets”) instituted; a web supplement houses useful but noncritical tables/figures.
  - This publication includes an abbreviated Preamble; detailed version online. The executive summary primarily contains the recommendations, and readers are encouraged to consult the full text for additional guidance and details.

2) Executive Summary
- Citation: 2018 AHA/ACC/AACVPR/AAPA/ABC/ACPM/ADA/AGS/APhA/ASPC/NLA/PCNA Guideline on the Management of Blood Cholesterol: Executive Summary. DOI: 10.1161/CIR.0000000000000624. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/30565953/ (Published 2019-01-01).
- Content focus: Condensed presentation of the guideline’s recommendations on blood cholesterol management developed by the ACC/AHA Task Force on Clinical Practice Guidelines with the same multi-society collaboration as the full guideline.
- Role: Serves as a concise, user-oriented summary emphasizing the recommendations themselves; readers should consult the full guideline for detailed rationale, supportive text, and supplemental materials.

Key themes across both publications
- Evidence-based, systematically developed recommendations to reduce CVD risk via cholesterol management.
- Emphasis on clinician-patient shared decision-making to tailor interventions.
- Clear structure and accessibility enhancements (modular format, hyperlinks, web supplements) to improve real-world implementation.
- Strong governance, transparency, and diverse expert input to minimize bias and enhance applicability.
