Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'ACMG-AMP 2015 guidelines variant interpretation genetics', 'max_results': 10} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 3777 tokens): Summary of included papers

Cross-cutting themes
- Broad movement from generic ACMG/AMP 2015 criteria toward gene- and disease-specific specifications led by ClinGen Variant Curation Expert Panels (VCEPs), improving concordance, resolving variants of uncertain significance (VUS), and reducing interpretation discrepancies.
- Calibration of evidence strength (e.g., refined population thresholds, tailored functional/segregation criteria, phenotype specificity) is central to improved classifications.
- Whole-genome sequencing (WGS) addresses limitations of exome sequencing by detecting deep intronic and structural variants, improving diagnostic yield in select cohorts.
- Emerging computational phenotyping (e.g., facial image analysis) and refined Bayesian frameworks can strengthen phenotype-based evidence (PP4).

Per-paper highlights

1) Whole Genome Sequencing in suspected inborn errors of immunity (IEIs) (Lee et al., 2025; doi: 10.1007/s10875-025-01947-2)
- Context: IEIs are >500 monogenic disorders; exome sequencing yields up to ~40% diagnoses but misses deep intronic/structural variants.
- Study: Applied WGS to 25 families with suspected IEIs to assess diagnostic yield and detect novel structural genomic alterations beyond exome capabilities.
- Key message: WGS is positioned to close gaps left by exome sequencing, particularly for intronic/structural variation.

2) PALB2 gene-specific ACMG/AMP specifications (Richardson et al., 2025; doi: 10.1016/j.ajhg.2025.08.020)
- Developed by the Hereditary Breast, Ovarian, and Pancreatic Cancer VCEP (HBOP VCEP).
- Actions: Advised against using 13 ACMG/AMP codes, limited 6, and tailored 9 to produce PALB2-specific guidelines.
- Pilot test: 39 variants (37 in ClinVar); 31/37 (84%) produced concordant classifications. Among 14 VUS/conflicting ClinVar entries, 4 were resolved under the new specs, aided by code combination changes and refined population cutoffs.
- Takeaway: Conservative, harmonized classifications with improved concordance versus existing ClinVar entries.

3) HHT gene-specific variant classification (DeMille et al., 2024; doi: 10.1155/2024/3043736)
- ClinGen HHT VCEP produced HHT-specific ACMG/AMP criteria recognizing disease- and gene-specific needs for ENG/ACVRL1/SMAD4-related Hereditary Hemorrhagic Telangiectasia.
- Pilot: Applied to 30 variants; demonstrates feasibility and standardization benefits for HHT variant interpretation.

4) Reclassification of VUS and role of gene-specific specifications (Benet-Pagès et al., 2025; doi: 10.1016/j.gimo.2024.101961)
- Emphasizes VUS as a major barrier to clinical utility.
- Highlights ClinGen-led, VCEP-implemented gene-specific adaptations to ACMG/AMP to reduce VUS.
- Notes recent ENIGMA VCEP specifications as an exemplar for improved classification; overall message supports ongoing, evidence-based, gene-specific refinements.

5) FBN1/Marfan syndrome specifications (Drackley et al., 2024; doi: 10.1186/s13073-024-01423-3)
- ClinGen FBN1 VCEP (est. 2018) tailored ACMG/AMP for FBN1 in Marfan syndrome to reduce discrepancies.
- Provides consensus recommendations addressing gene/disease-specific evidence (e.g., phenotype specificity, mutational hotspots, functional data).

6) Impact of ClinGen revisions vs ACMG-2015 in rare diseases (Rius et al., 2025; doi: 10.1016/j.cca.2024.120065)
- Comparative analysis of semi-automated classification using ClinGen-Rev vs ACMG-2015 on sets of variants from VCFs.
- Findings: 8.0% change in clinical verdicts; ClinGen-Rev correctly categorized 89.2% of curated variants vs 65.6% under ACMG-2015.
- Real-world simulation: Significant reduction in VUS and potential reduction in analysis time.
- Implication: ClinGen revisions enhance rigor, reproducibility, and efficiency.

7) Novel findings (Wu et al., 2024; doi: 10.3389/fgene.2024.1433060)
- Abstract text incomplete; details not available from provided content.

8) ENIGMA BRCA1/2 VCEP specifications (Parsons et al., 2024; doi: 10.1016/j.ajhg.2024.07.013)
