  Summary: Defines molecular tumor boards (MTBs) as multidisciplinary meetings that interpret biomarker results to guide personalized therapy. Highlights global disparities in MTB implementation driven by unequal access to molecular diagnostics and needed expertise.
  Implication: Calls attention to gaps in infrastructure and expertise that limit equitable precision oncology.

- AlphaMissense for Identifying Pathogenic Missense Mutations in DNA Damage Repair Genes in Cancer (PMID: 40570257; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1200/PO-24-00908)
  Summary: Evaluates the clinical accuracy of AlphaMissense, an AI tool predicting missense variant pathogenicity, specifically in DNA damage repair (DDR) genes using genomic and clinical features.
  Implication: Addresses whether AI predictions can be directly used in clinical decision-making; real-world validity requires assessment.

- Prevalence and clinical relavance of germline mutations in Chinese patients with pancreatic cancer (PMID: 40544258; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1186/s12964-025-02281-1)
  Summary: Investigates prevalence and clinical significance of germline sequence variants in unselected Chinese pancreatic cancer patients, an underexplored area.
  Implication: Aims to inform genetic testing strategies and management in this population.

- Genomic landscape and potential therapeutic targets in alpha-fetoprotein-producing gastric cancer (PMID: 39928247; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1007/s10120-025-01594-x)
  Design: Retrospective cohort (Shanxi Cancer Hospital, 2018–2020) of gastric cancer with elevated serum AFP; 91 patients underwent IHC, FISH, and NGS.
  Key findings:
  - AFPGC is more common in males; primarily located in cardia and antrum.
  - Diagnostic IHC panel: AFP, GPC3, SALL4, CD10, CDX-2, ATBF1.
  - NGS: TP53 hypermutation most frequent; altered pathways include signal transduction, RTK signaling, and second messenger pathways.
  - Actionability (OncoKB): cumulative incidence across 21 clinically actionable genes was 59.34%; CCNE1, ERBB2, EGFR most frequent.
  - Prognostic markers: LRP1B associated with overall survival; ARID1A with disease-free survival.
  Implication: Supports targeted therapy exploration and refined diagnostics/prognostication in AFPGC.

- Genetic Ancestry-Based Differences in Biomarker-Based Eligibility for Precision Oncology Therapies (PMID: 39786754; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1001/jamaoncol.2024.5794)
  Summary: Examines clinical implications of ancestry-associated differences in somatic mutation prevalence for eligibility to biomarker-driven therapies.
  Implication: Highlights potential inequities in access to precision treatments based on ancestry-linked biomarker distributions.

- Actionable mutations in early-stage ovarian cancer according to ESCAT: a descriptive analysis on a large prospective cohort (PMID: 39705839; 2025-01-01; doi:10.1016/j.esmoop.2024.104090)
  Context: Current ESMO algorithms for early-stage epithelial ovarian carcinoma rely on grade/histotype; adjuvant chemo is standard for high-grade and FIGO IB–IC, with concerns of overtreatment and unmet needs for higher-risk patients.
  Summary: Describes actionable mutations using the ESMO ESCAT framework in early-stage disease to refine therapeutic strategies.
  Implication: Seeks biomarkers to personalize adjuvant therapy and reduce overtreatment.

