  - Overview: NCBI provides search and retrieval across 35 distinct databases (e.g., GenBank for nucleic acid sequences; PubMed for citations/abstracts). E-utilities offer programmatic access to most databases.
  - New resources:
    - Comparative Genome Resource (CGR).
    - BLAST ClusteredNR database.
  - Major updates (past year) include: PubMed, PMC, Bookshelf, IgBLAST, Genome Data Viewer (GDV), RefSeq, NCBI Virus, GenBank type assemblies, iCn3D, ClinVar, Genetic Testing Registry (GTR), dbGaP, ALFA, ClinicalTrials.gov, Pathogen Detection, antimicrobial resistance resources, and PubChem.

- The new uORFdb: integrating literature, sequence, and variation data in a central hub for uORF research (Manske et al.; Nucleic Acids Research; doi:10.1093/nar/gkac899; published 2023-01-01; https://www.bioinformatics.uni-muenster.de/tools/uorfdb; PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36305828/)
  - Context: Upstream open reading frames (uORFs), initiated by AUG or near-cognate codons in transcript leader sequences, are widespread in eukaryotic transcripts, regulate downstream translation, can produce non-canonical peptides, and are implicated in diseases (including cancer).
  - Update from 2014 literature-focused release to a sequence-based platform:
    - Direct access to sequence information, graphical displays, and genetic variation data for >2.4 million human uORFs.
    - Includes >4.2 million uORFs across 12 additional species.
    - Visualization of multiple uORFs in transcript- and reading-frame-specific models to clarify translational context.
    - Rich filters, sequence metadata, and links to external resources (UCSC Genome Browser, dbSNP, ClinVar) for immediate in-depth analysis.
    - Somatic variation dataset: uORF-related variants from WGS of 677 TCGA cancer samples.


