Tool `Paper Search:search_pubmed` with Parameter {'query': 'PRISMA 2020 statement BMJ 2021', 'max_results': 5} on Paper Search succeeded. Result (summarized from 1214 tokens): SUMMARIZED CONTENT:
- Ossified spinal epidermoid cyst: A systematic review and case report. (Hovis GEA; Chandla A; Kolker SE; Yang I; Nagasawa DT). Heliyon, 2024-01-01. DOI: 10.1016/j.heliyon.2024.e37093. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/39315203/
  - Topic: Epidermoid cysts (ECs) are rare, benign spinal lesions (<1% of spinal tumors). Calcification is rare; ossification in the lumbar spine had not been documented previously.
  - Contribution: First known case report of a congenital lumbar intradural epidermoid cyst with ossification, plus a systematic literature review of intradural lumbar ECs.
  - Significance: Documents an unprecedented ossified lumbar EC presentation and synthesizes prior intradural lumbar EC evidence.

- TDCS at home for depressive disorders: an updated systematic review and lessons learned from a prematurely terminated randomized controlled pilot study. (Kumpf U; Palm U; Eder J; Ezim H; Stadler M; Burkhardt G; Dechantsreiter E; Padberg F). Eur Arch Psychiatry Clin Neurosci, 2023-01-01. DOI: 10.1007/s00406-023-01620-y. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/37191697/
  - Context: Home-based transcranial direct current stimulation (tDCS) for major depressive disorder (MDD) is attractive due to safety profile, cost-effectiveness, and scalability.
  - Systematic review: Summarizes available studies on at-home tDCS for MDD, identifying critical safety and implementation issues that require attention.
  - RCT (HomeDC) design: Double-blind, placebo-controlled, parallel-group pilot. Adults with MDD (DSM-5) randomized to active or sham tDCS. Protocol: 6 weeks, 5 sessions/week, 30 min at 2 mA; montage anode F3, cathode F4. Sham mimicked ramp-in/out without ongoing stimulation.
  - Outcome: Trial was prematurely terminated due to accumulated adverse events (skin lesions). Only 11 participants enrolled. Feasibility of home delivery was good, but safety monitoring was insufficient to detect/prevent AEs promptly.
  - Efficacy: Depression scores decreased over time in both groups; active tDCS was not superior to sham.
  - Implication: Home tDCS/TES has promise but requires robust safety monitoring and further high-quality RCTs.

- Mental health literacy of school-going adolescents in sub-Saharan Africa: a regional systematic review protocol. (Sodi T; Quarshie EN; Oppong Asante K; Radzilani-Makatu M; Makgahlela M; Nkoana S; Mutambara J). BMJ Open, 2022-01-01. DOI: 10.1136/bmjopen-2022-063687. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/36127093/
  - Rationale: Mental health literacy (MHL) influences identification and treatment of mental health problems; adolescents are a key target group. Most MHL evidence comes from high-income countries.
  - Objective: Synthesize published primary evidence on the status and measurement of MHL among school-going adolescents in sub-Saharan Africa.
  - Note: Protocol paper outlining planned methodology to address a regional evidence gap.

- Applied sports science and sports medicine in women's rugby: systematic scoping review and Delphi study to establish future research priorities. (Heyward O; Emmonds S; Roe G; Scantlebury S; Stokes K; Jones B). BMJ Open Sport Exerc Med, 2022-01-01. DOI: 10.1136/bmjsem-2021-001287. PubMed: https://pubmed.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/35979431/
  - Aim: Part 1—conduct a systematic scoping review of applied sports science and sports medicine in women’s rugby. Part 2—use a Delphi process to develop a consensus statement on future research priorities.
  - Contribution: Maps existing evidence and establishes a research agenda to guide future work in women’s rugby performance and health.
