What this means for impact risk
- None of the listed objects are predicted to impact Earth in the next week. The closest pass, 2025 US6 at 0.415 lunar distances (159,496 km), is a meter‑class object and is not in the Sentry impact risk table. If a meter‑scale object were to enter the atmosphere, it would typically disintegrate as a fireball; however, there is no impact solution reported for this week’s approaches.
- For verification, each object’s JPL SBDB page (links above) shows hazard status and historical/next close approach distances; the weekly list is from NASA’s NEO feed for 2025‑12‑01…08 (used to compile the figures above).

2) Solar activity context and whether it changes the risk

- Major flare/CME on 2025‑12‑01:
  • GOES reported an X1.9 flare beginning 02:27Z, peaking 02:49Z, at location ~N23E90.
    DONKI flare record: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/FLR/ (event ID 2025‑12‑01T02:27:00‑FLR‑001; also referenced in CME below).
  • Associated CME observed as a halo from the east limb; DONKI analyses list speeds 1837–1903 km/s.
    DONKI CME: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/CME/43155/-1
    Notes: “Space Weather Notification – CME (Mars)” was issued for this activity on 2025‑12‑01, indicating modeled impact away from Earth. WSA‑Enlil simulation runs from 2025‑11‑28 also show CME arrivals at Parker Solar Probe and at Mars on 2025‑12‑02 08:00Z (Simulation ID WSA‑ENLIL/43080/1).
- Recent geomagnetic context (for perspective, earlier in November):
  • 2025‑11‑12 severe geomagnetic storm: Kp 8.67 and 8.33 reported.
    DONKI GST: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42642/-1 and https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42662/-1
  • 2025‑11‑06 moderate storm: Kp 6.0–6.67.
    DONKI GST: https://webtools.ccmc.gsfc.nasa.gov/DONKI/view/GST/42455/-1
- Relevance to asteroid risk: This week’s X‑class CME originated near the east limb (E90) and was modeled/flagged toward Mars, not Earth. There is no evidence in DONKI of an Earth‑directed CME from this event, and no impact alerts that would affect asteroid trajectories. (Asteroid ephemerides and flyby geometries are determined from celestial mechanics and tracking; solar CMEs do not suddenly redirect asteroids on week‑timescales.)

3) “Recent imagery of Earth around where these asteroids might hit”

- There are no predicted impact locations for this week because all listed objects are flybys with positive miss distances (see above). Therefore there is no “impact zone” to image.
- Satellite imagery status: Attempts to fetch latest DSCOVR/EPIC Earth images and NASA Earth Imagery tiles during this session returned HTTP 503 errors (service temporarily unavailable). Example: EPIC natural/enhanced for 2025‑12‑01 and 2025‑12‑02 returned 503.
  • EPIC API status: multiple 503 responses while querying 2025‑11‑30 to 2025‑12‑02.
- If you need current Earth imagery for general situational awareness (e.g., sky conditions for fireball observing), use these official, public sources:
  • NASA Worldview (global true‑color from MODIS/VIIRS/GOES/Himawari): https://worldview.earthdata.nasa.gov
  • NOAA GOES-East/West imagery: https://www.star.nesdis.noaa.gov/goes/
  • EUMETSAT imagery: https://view.eumetsat.int
These provide verifiable, timestamped imagery even when the EPIC API is undergoing maintenance.

4) Notes on data and uncertainties

- Diameters are estimated from absolute magnitude with assumed albedo ranges; that’s why each object shows a min–max size range on JPL SBDB.
- Miss distances reported above are the nominal minimum separation at closest approach (center‑to‑center distance from Earth). For context, 1 lunar distance (LD) = 384,400 km; I’ve provided the LD equivalents for the close passes.

